            {
              "option": "Move PASS 1 to a worker_threads pool now",
              "rejected_because": "Requires FactLookup serialization helpers, a pool abstraction, and lifecycle plumbing for cancellation/cleanup. Disproportionate cost without evidence of CPU saturation."
            },
            {
              "option": "Stream candidates through an async generator",
              "rejected_because": "Three passes need full-batch cross-references (PASS 3 reads edgeAssessments produced by PASS 1; semantic boost runs over the whole list). Generator forces refactoring multi-pass shape with no fairness gain over chunked yields."
            },
            {
              "option": "Yield every iteration",
              "rejected_because": "Per-iteration yields balloon the microtask queue and add measurable overhead with no responsiveness benefit beyond yielding every ~100."
            },
            {
              "option": "Configurable batch size in cognitive tuning",
              "rejected_because": "100 is a reasonable default; exposing another knob is premature. Constant can be promoted to tuning if a profile ever needs a different value."
            }
          ]
        },
        "consequences": {
          "expected": [
            "A normalization cycle over a 1 000-edge dream graph yields ~30 times across all three passes, letting the daemon service HTTP and dashboard traffic between chunks instead of blocking",
            "Wall-clock time per cycle increases marginally (one event-loop tick per yield, sub-ms each); throughput essentially unchanged",
            "Purely structural change — no data migration, no behaviour change for any input, no new persistence",
            "TODO referencing this ADR creates a clear escalation path if profiling later shows CPU saturation: convert PASS 1 to a worker thread without revisiting the basic batching pattern"
          ],
          "risks": [
            "Concurrent mutations of dreamGraph from other code paths during yield windows would now be observable. The engine's NORMALIZING state guard prevents this, but the assumption is now load-bearing in a way it wasn't before. If that guard is ever weakened, the yields become a correctness hazard.",
            "The 100-iteration constant is hand-tuned. Pathologically uneven validation costs (e.g. one extremely heavy LLM-augmented edge in a chunk) could still produce a noticeable pause inside a chunk. If observed, escalate to the worker-thread plan rather than shrinking the batch."
          ]
        },
        "guard_rails": [
          "Do NOT remove the `await yieldToEventLoop()` calls from the three passes. They are the F-12 fix; the daemon will block on large dream graphs without them.",
          "Do NOT mutate dreamGraph, edgeAssessments, or lookup from any other code path while normalization is running. The engine's NORMALIZING state guard enforces this and is now load-bearing for correctness.",
          "Do NOT shrink NORMALIZER_YIELD_BATCH below 25 or expand it above 500 without profiling evidence. The current value is a deliberate trade-off between fairness and overhead.",
          "When the worker-thread follow-up is implemented, mark this ADR as superseded with a forward link to the new ADR. Do NOT silently delete the batching code — keep it as the single-thread fallback path."
        ],
        "tags": [
          "cognitive",
