        "id": "ADR-099",
        "title": "Progressive ADR enforcement (advisory → soft → strict)",
        "date": "2026-05-03T10:27:16.749Z",
        "decided_by": "human",
        "status": "accepted",
        "context": {
          "problem": "ADRs are currently paper. discoverAndRecordADRs() is never invoked, the normalizer has no ADR guard rails, and the architect only consults ADRs when the user prompt matches a regex. Code changes can silently violate accepted ADRs. Hard-blocking by default would create user resentment and obstruct legitimate exception cases.",
          "constraints": [
            "Humans must always retain manual override capability",
            "Default behavior must build trust, not friction",
            "Teams with stricter policy needs must have an opt-in escalation path",
            "Enforcement level must be configurable per instance"
          ],
          "affected_entities": [
            "src/cognitive/normalizer.ts",
            "src/tools/record-architecture-decision.ts",
            "extensions/vscode/src/context-builder.ts",
            "extensions/vscode/prompts/architect_core.md",
            "adr_log.json"
          ]
        },
        "decision": {
          "chosen": "Three progressive levels controlled by DREAMGRAPH_ADR_ENFORCEMENT=advisory|soft|strict (default: advisory). Advisory: log warning, create tension signal, explain violation risk in architect response — no blocking. Soft (workspace opt-in): architect surfaces a confirmation prompt before proceeding (\"This conflicts with ADR-004. Proceed anyway? [yes/no]\"). Strict (team setting): blocks AUTONOMOUS promotion only — normalizer refuses to promote ADR-violating edges and architect refuses to apply ADR-violating patches without an explicit manual override gesture. Humans may always override manually at every level.",
          "alternatives": [
            {
              "option": "Hard-block by default",
              "rejected_because": "Generates friction faster than it generates value; obstructs legitimate exception cases."
            },
            {
              "option": "Pure advisory only (no strict path)",
              "rejected_because": "Insufficient for teams with formal architecture compliance requirements."
            },
            {
              "option": "Per-ADR enforcement level",
              "rejected_because": "Configuration sprawl; project-wide policy is the right granularity for this version."
            }
          ]
        },
        "consequences": {
          "expected": [
            "ADRs become enforceable instead of decorative",
            "Default advisory mode builds user trust by explaining rather than blocking",
            "Soft mode adds friction only proportional to risk",
            "Strict mode satisfies team policy without locking out human judgment",
            "Per-instance config keeps the choice local to project risk tolerance"
          ],
          "risks": [
            "Soft-mode confirmation prompts may be ignored / clicked through habitually",
            "Strict mode could surprise users who didn't realize an ADR was in effect",
            "Tension signals from advisory mode could create alert fatigue"
          ]
        },
        "guard_rails": [
          "Manual human override MUST work at every enforcement level",
          "Default level MUST remain advisory — never escalate by default",
          "Strict mode MUST only block autonomous promotion, never block humans",
          "DREAMGRAPH_ADR_ENFORCEMENT env var name MUST NOT change without migration notes"
        ],
        "tags": [
          "adr",
          "guard-rails",
          "enforcement",
          "architect"
        ]
      },
