        "id": "ADR-125",
        "title": "LLM request timeout is a configurable LlmConfig field driven by env var",
        "date": "2026-05-05T12:04:19.016Z",
        "decided_by": "collaborative",
        "status": "accepted",
        "context": {
          "problem": "All LLM provider implementations had hardcoded request timeouts. Operators running larger local models that legitimately exceeded the default could not extend the budget without editing source. There was no environment-variable knob and no shared LlmConfig field for the timeout.",
          "constraints": [
            "Default behaviour must be preserved (120 s budget) for operators who do not set the env var",
            "All four provider constructors must accept the new parameter with a default value (backward source-compat)",
            "Liveness probes stay short-circuited; only the completion path honours the configured timeout",
            "The dashboard.ts LlmConfig literal must include timeoutMs so TypeScript stays clean"
          ],
          "affected_entities": [
            "src/cognitive/llm.ts",
            "src/server/dashboard.ts",
            "DREAMGRAPH_LLM_TIMEOUT_MS env var",
            "OllamaProvider",
            "OpenAiCompatibleProvider",
            "AnthropicProvider"
          ]
        },
        "decision": {
          "chosen": "Add LlmConfig.timeoutMs (number, ms). parseLlmConfig() reads DREAMGRAPH_LLM_TIMEOUT_MS from env and defaults to 120_000 ms when unset or invalid. initLlmProvider() threads c.timeoutMs into all four provider constructors (ollama, openai, lmstudio, anthropic). Each provider stores it as a private field and uses AbortSignal.timeout(this.timeoutMs) on the actual completion fetch. Liveness probes (3 s ollama / 5 s openai-compatible) intentionally retain hardcoded short timeouts \\u2014 they test reachability, not request budget.",
          "alternatives": []
        },
        "consequences": {
          "expected": [
            "Operators running large local models (e.g. 70B+) can extend the LLM request budget without code edits",
            "Default behaviour is unchanged; existing deployments need no migration",
            "Dashboard, scheduler, and dream cycle paths all share the same configurable timeout"
          ],
          "risks": [
            "Setting the env var to an unreasonably large value (e.g. hours) can mask a hung provider; operators must choose responsibly",
            "The cap is per request, not per session — a stuck provider can still consume budget across many calls",
            "Negative or zero values fall back to the 120_000 default; this is intentional but may surprise operators expecting an instant-fail mode"
          ]
        },
        "guard_rails": [
          "Do NOT hardcode timeouts in provider implementations — always accept timeoutMs and use AbortSignal.timeout(this.timeoutMs)",
          "Do NOT change the liveness-probe short timeouts to use the configured value; reachability and request-budget are different concerns",
          "If adding a new provider, accept timeoutMs as the last constructor parameter with default 120_000 to match existing providers",
          "The default (120_000) and the env var name (DREAMGRAPH_LLM_TIMEOUT_MS) are part of the operator contract — do not rename without a migration note"
        ],
        "tags": []
