        "decided_by": "collaborative",
        "status": "accepted",
        "context": {
          "problem": "v1's pass budget was optional \\u2014 `0` or `undefined` meant uncapped \\u2014 and there was no wall-clock bound at all. A pass that called a slow LLM could hang indefinitely; a model that issued many small operations could blow through resources without ever tripping a counter. The Slice 3 directive was explicit: prevent both failure modes by construction.",
          "constraints": [
            "v1's optional `totalAuthorizedPasses` (where 0 / undefined meant 'uncapped') is rejected as unsafe — the user directive at Slice 3 kickoff explicitly required dual bounded budgets.",
            "Counters must remain visible during runs (Slice 3 carry-over: 'visible counters as a product requirement').",
            "The decision engine must be able to detect exhaustion without performing I/O — it is given `nowEpochMs` as an input.",
            "Both budgets must be constructible without I/O so they can be created in tests."
          ],
          "affected_entities": [
            "extensions/vscode/src/architect-v2/autonomy/budget.ts",
            "extensions/vscode/src/architect-v2/autonomy/task.ts",
            "extensions/vscode/src/architect-v2/autonomy/decision.ts",
            "extensions/vscode/src/architect-v2/autonomy/modes.ts"
          ]
        },
        "decision": {
          "chosen": "Every Architect v2 autonomous run is bounded by BOTH a `PassBudget` AND a `TimeBudget`. `PassBudget.total` is REQUIRED on every run \\u2014 there is no uncapped mode. `TimeBudget.totalMs` is defaulted per `ModeProfile.defaultTimeBudgetMs` and overrideable per run. The decision engine stops on whichever exhausts first, returning `{ kind: 'stop', condition: 'pass_budget_exhausted' }` or `{ kind: 'stop', condition: 'time_budget_exhausted' }` respectively. Both budgets expose `total` / `used` / `remaining` so the UI can display live counters at all times. `createPassBudget(0)` and `createPassBudget(NaN)` throw \\u2014 there is no way to construct an unbounded run.",
          "alternatives": [
            {
              "option": "Pass budget only (v1 model)",
              "rejected_because": "Does not bound long-running stalls — a single pass that hangs on a slow provider call can run indefinitely while consuming zero budget. v1 had no defense against this."
            },
            {
              "option": "Time budget only",
              "rejected_because": "Doesn't bound runaway tool-call loops within a single time window. Wall-clock alone allows pathological retry storms."
            },
            {
              "option": "Optional budgets with sensible defaults",
              "rejected_because": "Optional means default-on which means users may not realize a long-running task is bounded; explicit-required matches the safety posture this rewrite is trying to establish."
            }
          ]
        },
        "consequences": {
          "expected": [
            "Infinite loops and long stalls are structurally precluded — the decision engine cannot return `{ kind: 'continue' }` past either budget.",
