        "id": "ADR-154",
        "title": "Continuation prompts MUST embed the exact selected tool name; createContinuationNeed() enforces at construction",
        "date": "2026-05-09T21:18:40.427Z",
        "decided_by": "collaborative",
        "status": "accepted",
        "context": {
          "problem": "In v1, continuation prompts described the next step in natural language without reliably embedding the exact tool name. Models would then continue using a near-synonym tool from the available set (e.g. `read_file` instead of `read_source_code`) and silently fail or do the wrong thing. The user directive at Slice 3 kickoff specifically called this out: 'continuation prompt must include proper keywords or actual tool names to absolutely ensure correct tool selection.'",
          "constraints": [
            "The selected tool name must reach the model in unmistakable form, not as a label or paraphrase.",
            "Enforcement must be at construction time so violations cannot be silently introduced anywhere downstream.",
            "The prompt must also list considered alternatives so the model can recover gracefully if the top choice fails (without re-discovering the option set)."
          ],
          "affected_entities": [
            "extensions/vscode/src/architect-v2/autonomy/signals.ts",
            "extensions/vscode/src/architect-v2/autonomy/decision.ts"
          ]
        },
        "decision": {
          "chosen": "Every `ContinuationNeed` produced by the autonomy decision engine MUST embed the exact MCP/local tool name of `selectedAction.tool` verbatim in `continuationPrompt`. This is enforced at construction: `createContinuationNeed()` throws if `continuationPrompt.includes(selectedAction.tool) === false`. `buildContinuationPrompt(top, alternatives)` produces a string that includes the tool name in backticks plus a brief mention of considered alternatives. Direct construction of `ContinuationNeed` literals that bypass `createContinuationNeed()` is forbidden by guard rail.",
          "alternatives": [
            {
              "option": "Free-form continuation prompts (v1 model)",
              "rejected_because": "v1's `buildContinuationPrompt` produced strings like 'Continue with the recommended next step: <label>' with the tool name only as an optional hint. Models routinely pick a near-synonym tool when the exact name is not embedded; this is the failure mode ADR-154 exists to remove."
            },
            {
              "option": "Pass tools as a separate structured field instead of embedding in the prompt",
              "rejected_because": "The provider's tool-call dispatcher receives the `tools` array regardless, but the model's free-text reasoning is what selects WHICH tool to call. Embedding the name in the prompt anchors that selection. Both mechanisms together (tools array + prompt embed) is defense in depth."
            },
            {
              "option": "Validate at the LLM-call boundary instead of at construction",
              "rejected_because": "Defers the failure to a place where it's harder to attribute and easier to silently work around. Construction-time enforcement makes the violation visible at the source."
            }
          ]
        },
        "consequences": {
          "expected": [
            "The class of failure where the model continues with `read_file` instead of `read_source_code` (or similar near-synonym substitution) is prevented at the prompt layer.",
            "Action producers must populate `ActionCandidate.tool` with the exact registered tool name, not a friendly label — this is now enforced.",
            "Continuation prompts have a recognizable shape that includes both the chosen tool and the reasoning, useful for telemetry and replay.",
            "Tests can assert prompt content includes specific tool names without brittle regex."
