        "id": "ADR-193",
        "title": "Keep Architect core normalized while classifying MCP tools by authority",
        "date": "2026-05-14T09:04:52.206Z",
        "decided_by": "collaborative",
        "status": "accepted",
        "context": {
          "problem": "CLI agents such as Codex CLI, Copilot CLI, and Claude CLI can be configured with MCP servers and generic/provider tools. Architect needs to use DreamGraph tools as authoritative system capabilities without letting provider-native inline tools or arbitrary MCP tools redefine Architect core contracts or become untrusted sources of truth.",
          "constraints": [
            "Architect core may speak only ArchitectRunRequest, ArchitectRunResult, and ArchitectPassEnvelope.",
            "Provider-specific requests, transcripts, process captures, MCP configs, and tool outputs must remain adapter-private until normalized.",
            "DreamGraph tools may be exposed to CLI agents via MCP, but their outputs must still be policy-checked and normalized before Architect core consumes them.",
            "Generic MCP tools and provider inline tools may be useful for context or local actions, but they must not be treated as authoritative DreamGraph facts unless revalidated by DreamGraph.",
            "CLI implementations differ: Copilot CLI uses ~/.copilot/mcp-config.json and per-server tools allowlists; Codex CLI uses ~/.codex/config.toml / project config with mcp_servers allowlists, required flags, timeouts, and sandbox/approval controls; Claude CLI/Claude Code MCP support must be handled through its own adapter policy."
          ],
          "affected_entities": [
            "ArchitectRunRequest",
            "ArchitectRunResult",
            "ArchitectPassEnvelope",
            "CliAgentAdapter",
            "DreamGraph MCP server",
            "codex-cli",
            "copilot-cli",
            "claude-cli",
            "OpenAI API adapter",
            "Anthropic API adapter",
            "ChatGPT manual bridge",
            "Claude manual bridge"
          ],
          "related_tensions": [
            "authoritative-tools-vs-provider-tools",
            "adapter-normalization-vs-core-contamination",
            "subscription-cli-surface-vs-api-surface"
          ]
        },
        "decision": {
          "chosen": "Classify MCP/tool access into authority tiers. DreamGraph-authoritative MCP tools are the only tool outputs that can become authoritative Architect evidence, and only after adapter readiness checks, allowlist verification, transcript capture, and normalization into ArchitectRunResult. Generic MCP tools and provider inline tools are treated as contextual or action-producing provider-local capabilities; their outputs can be quoted in adapter evidence but cannot mutate Architect state or satisfy authoritative graph/tool requirements unless revalidated through DreamGraph. Architect core remains provider-agnostic and receives only ArchitectRunRequest, ArchitectRunResult, and ArchitectPassEnvelope.",
          "alternatives": [
            {
              "option": "Let each CLI expose arbitrary MCP and inline tools directly to Architect core.",
              "rejected_because": "This would couple Architect to provider-specific tool semantics and allow untrusted/generic tools to become de facto authoritative."
            },
            {
              "option": "Disable all MCP support for CLI agents.",
              "rejected_because": "This wastes the strongest integration path for giving subscription/CLI agents controlled DreamGraph context and tool access."
            },
            {
              "option": "Treat all MCP servers equally if configured by the user.",
              "rejected_because": "User configuration alone does not establish authority, provenance, normalization, or compatibility with Architect contracts."
            }
          ]
        },
        "consequences": {
          "expected": [
            "Adapters must generate or validate CLI-specific MCP configuration before authoritative runs.",
            "Adapters must capture MCP/tool transcripts sufficient for audit and normalization.",
            "Routing can fail closed when an authoritative DreamGraph operation is requested but the CLI adapter cannot prove DreamGraph MCP tools are available under policy.",
            "Provider inline tools remain useful but are never considered a substitute for DreamGraph-authoritative MCP tools.",
            "The same ArchitectRunResult contract can represent manual bridge, CLI, and API outputs without changing Architect core."
          ],
          "risks": [
            "CLI MCP configuration formats and flags may change across versions.",
            "Some CLIs may not support run-scoped MCP config cleanly, requiring profile isolation or temporary config directories.",
            "Generic MCP/tool output can still influence model text unless prompts and adapters label authority boundaries clearly.",
            "Provider CLIs may auto-select tools, making strict transcript capture and tool allowlists important."
          ]
        },
        "guard_rails": [
          "Do not add provider-specific request/result types to Architect core.",
          "Do not treat generic MCP or provider inline tool output as authoritative DreamGraph facts without revalidation.",
          "Do not route authoritative Architect operations to a CLI adapter unless DreamGraph MCP readiness and allowlists pass.",
          "Keep CLI process captures, MCP configs, and provider-native transcripts adapter-private except as normalized evidence fields in ArchitectRunResult.",
          "Prefer fail-closed behavior for missing or ambiguous MCP authority configuration."
        ],
        "tags": [
          "architect",
          "mcp",
          "cli-agent",
          "normalization",
          "dreamgraph-authority",
          "codex-cli",
          "copilot-cli",
          "claude-cli"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "ADR-194",
        "title": "Architect agentic loop: write-pressure reservation is anchor-gated and auto-lifts on any write",
        "date": "2026-05-15T23:01:59.001Z",
        "decided_by": "collaborative",
        "status": "accepted",
        "context": {
          "problem": "The Architect's task-level write-pressure mechanism in `runAgenticLoop` (`extensions/vscode/src/chat-panel.ts`) had two failure modes observable at runtime: (1) once tripped, the reservation never lifted within the loop even after the model successfully wrote, causing the model to paraphrase the constraint as a permanent \"no more reads\" persona that bled into subsequent turns; (2) the reservation armed on lifetime read count regardless of task type, so read-only or assess-only user prompts (which inherently must read) were getting blocked from reading. A user-reported case showed the Architect refusing to read the body of a Copilot CLI provider file it had been asked to investigate, citing a \"no more reads\" constraint that did not match the user's intent.",
          "constraints": [
            "Never-fail invariant: the loop must keep serving the Architect; constraints are gravity hints, not hard router gates.",
            "Provider-agnostic: the rule must work for any architect provider/model — no provider-specific branching.",
            "Token economy: must still apply real pressure when the prior pass established a sticky patch anchor and the model is wasting tokens re-locating the same anchor.",
            "Common-sense reset: any write of any kind (host-local writes, MCP writes, markdown chapter patches, entity edits, run_command, etc.) must reset the constraint — not just a hard-coded subset.",
            "Read/assess tasks and fresh user prompts must not be subject to write-pressure at all."
          ],
          "affected_entities": [
            "extensions/vscode/src/chat-panel.ts",
            "extensions/vscode/src/autonomy.ts",
            "extensions/vscode/src/autonomy-loop.ts",
            "extensions/vscode/src/tool-classification.ts"
          ]
        },
        "decision": {
          "chosen": "In `runAgenticLoop`, gate the write-reservation on `AutonomyState.lastAnchorPaths` being non-empty (a sticky patch anchor established by a prior autonomy pass), track `readsSinceLastWrite` instead of lifetime reads, and lift the reservation automatically the first time any non-read-prefixed tool runs in the loop — emitting a synthetic user message that announces the lift. The reservation may re-arm later in the same loop if the model returns to consecutive reads without writing. The reservation text now explicitly enumerates allowed reads under pressure (exact body for safe patch, post-write verification, stale-anchor recovery, reading a newly identified concrete blocker target) versus discouraged reads (broad search, re-reading already-inspected files, relocating the same anchor, \"just to be sure\" discovery). The wrap-up \"you spent the budget on reads\" framing is gated by the same anchor signal so read-only turns get the generic wrap-up regardless of read volume.",
          "alternatives": [
            {
              "option": "Keep the original one-shot lifetime-read reservation",
              "rejected_because": "Caused the runtime regression where the Architect refused to read on inherently read-only tasks and held the constraint across turns."
            },
            {
              "option": "Hard-strip read tools from the catalog after threshold",
              "rejected_because": "Violates the never-fail / no-firewall invariant; the router must never reject reads mid-turn — pressure is gravity-driven, not gate-driven."
            },
            {
              "option": "Lift the reservation only on a hard-coded subset of write tools (e.g. replace_string_in_file, multi_replace_string_in_file)",
              "rejected_because": "Provider-agnostic and surface-agnostic resets are required: patch_markdown_chapter, edit_markdown_section, edit_entity, create_file, run_command, MCP-prefixed write tools, etc. all represent legitimate progress and must reset the counter. The existing `isReadOnlyTool` prefix classifier in `autonomy-loop.ts` already provides the canonical read/non-read split."
            }
          ]
        },
        "consequences": {
          "expected": [
            "Read-only/assess user prompts read freely; the reservation never arms when no prior-pass anchor exists.",
            "When the prior pass established an anchor, write-pressure still applies — but lifts the moment the model writes anything (including markdown chapter patches), and may re-arm if the model goes back to consecutive reads.",
            "The Architect no longer paraphrases the reservation as a persistent 'no more reads' persona, because the synthetic user message explicitly states it is not a permanent ban and is automatically lifted on the next write.",
            "Wrap-up messaging matches the task type: read-only turns get the generic summary, anchor-bound write-stalled turns get the strict 'name the patch or the prerequisite' framing."
          ],
          "risks": [
            "The reservation depends on `AutonomyState.lastAnchorPaths` being populated by the prior pass via `extractProseAnchorPaths` and the autonomy state advance — if that signal regresses (false negatives), write-pressure will under-fire on legitimate stalls.",
            "The `isReadOnlyTool` prefix list in `autonomy-loop.ts` is the classification source of truth; new read-only tools added without that prefix would be miscounted as writes and would prematurely lift the reservation. Mitigation: keep the prefix list as the canonical taxonomy and extend it when new read-only tools are introduced."
          ]
        },
        "guard_rails": [
          "Do not re-introduce a hard-strip / firewall that removes read tools from the catalog after threshold — pressure is gravity only.",
          "Do not gate the reservation on lifetime read count or on a per-turn one-shot flag — `readsSinceLastWrite` plus auto-lift on any write is the contract.",
          "Do not arm the reservation in the absence of `AutonomyState.lastAnchorPaths` — read-only and assess tasks must read freely.",
          "Do not narrow the auto-lift to a hard-coded subset of write tools — any non-read-prefixed tool counts as a write for reset purposes."
        ],
        "tags": [
          "architect",
          "autonomy",
          "token-economy",
          "agentic-loop",
          "write-pressure",
          "gravity-not-firewall",
          "chat-panel",
          "runtime-fix"
        ]
      },
